how do the results of this presidential  election reflect the will of the people  since more people voted for Hillary  Clinton than for Donald Trump and as the  votes continue to be counted we see that  in 2016 like in the year 2000 the  Democratic presidential candidate  received more votes in the popular vote  but ultimately lost the electoral vote  this is a screenshot from the New York  Times election tracker earlier today and  as you see Hillary Clinton receiving  about two hundred and thirty ish  thousand more votes than Donald Trump  today Donald Trump with more electoral  votes than Hillary Clinton but about a  quarter million fewer votes overall this  is the system that gave us George W Bush  and Donald Trump in spite of more votes  for the other candidates Al Gore and  Hillary Clinton respectively and because  of how the Electoral College works it's  actually possible mathematically for a  candidate to win the White House with  under thirty percent of the popular vote  Danielle Kurtz Leben from NPR recently  reported on this we must abolish this  ridiculous system now right-wing nuts  are going to email me Pat and they're  gonna say David you're saying that  because Hillary lost the election and  you just want to help Democrats well no  that's why I'm reminding you about it  today but I've been saying this for  years I've been talking about it for  nearly twenty years since I learned  about this harebrained system in middle  school the Electoral College a college  was established in 1787 and there's a  couple reasons why it was established  one of which was to put the power of the  election officially in the hands of  electors who I guess might be more  trustworthy conceivably than a national  popular vote and sort of create these 51  mini elections and another big part of  it was when you take away the popular  vote is the deciding a barometer for  elections you take power away from  population centers because if you have a  national popular vote candidates are  going to go where there are more people  they're going to go to the big cities  and they're going to ignore more rural  areas the problem is that by instituting  the electoral vote and set  this this primary system that we have  the two elements together have just  created an opposite problem where the  candidates rarely go to the population  centers and all of those who live in  cities are by and large ignored and  instead the candidates focus on  arbitrarily important states like Nevada  Ohio I'm sorry not Ohio New Hampshire  Iowa Nevada etc so I understand the  IdeaPad but it's created the exact same  problem just for a different group of  people and it's now happened four times  in American history that the winner of  the electoral vote actually lost the  popular vote meaning that less people  wanted them to become president yeah but  really it's not a partisan issue there's  usually not much to do about it in  Congress so I don't see it changing  anytime soon  I don't either and I'll talk to you  about what would happen for it to change  but I also want to mention one other  thing I mentioned that this electoral  college system was established in 1787  we didn't have media that allowed people  all over the country to see their  candidates if they didn't actually show  up in their town maybe you would  occasionally read about it in a  newspaper I am NOT less informed about  my choice in the 2016 election because I  didn't see the candidates speak in  person and that is something that we  have to think about much of the system  is more relevant the explanation for the  electoral college system makes more  sense when you can't just turn on your  TV no matter where you are or the radio  or the internet or open a newspaper and  see what it is that the candidates are  doing or saying now it's also not  democratic at the state level because  the Electoral College with the exception  of those two districts in Maine and the  two districts two or three districts 